at is the value of \n$\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}?$" 13194139536068 true] [17592186382065 1113 #uuid "b71065be-44ce-5f43-8458-63be59f4de8a" 13194139536068 true] [17592186382065 1145 "Recall that for any $2\\times 2$ real matrix $A$ with complex eigenvalues, we have $A=PC{P}^{-1},$ where \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}],\\,C=[\\begin{aligned}\\text{Re}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Im}(\\lambda ) \\\\ -\\text{Im}(\\lambda ) & \\text{Re}(\\lambda )\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nand $v$ is an eigenvector of $A$ corresponding to the complex eigenvalue  $\\lambda .$\n\nTo compute the eigenvalues of $A,$ we solve the characteristic equation, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A-\\lambda I) & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-3-\\lambda  & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+2\\lambda +5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the quadratic formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lambda  & =\\frac{-b\\pm \\sqrt{{b}^{2}-4ac}}{2a}=-1\\pm 2\\text{i}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the eigenvalue $\\lambda =-1+2\\text{i}.$ We can immediately construct the rotation-scaling matrix:\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo compute the eigenvectors of a matrix, we need to find non-zero solutions of the matrix equation \n$(A-\\lambda I)\\text{x}=0.$ \nIn our case, we have $\\,{\\lambda }_{1}=-1+2\\text{i},$ and\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\lambda I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -4 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]-[\\begin{aligned}-1+2\\text{i} & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1+2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-2-2\\text{i} & -4 \\\\ 2 & 2-2\\text{i}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the homogeneous system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}(-2-2\\text{i}){x}_{1}-4{x}_{2}=0 \\\\ 2{x}_{1}+(2-2\\text{i}){x}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the matrix $A-\\lambda I$ is singular, the system above has a non-trivial solution. This implies that one row is a multiple of another. As a result, we can drop one of the rows and take one of the variables as free. For example, from the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=-(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nHence, the general solution is given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-(1-\\text{i}){x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{aligned}],\\,{x}_{2}\\in C.\n$$\n\nSetting ${x}_{2}=-1$, we get the eigenvector \n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}1-\\text{i} \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can construct the matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =[\\begin{aligned}| & | \\\\ \\text{Re}(v) & \\text{Im}(v) \\\\ | & |\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}^{2}+{b}^{2}}{{c}^{2}+{d}^{2}}=\\frac{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}}{(-1)^{2}+{0}^{2}}=2.\n$$" 13194139536068 true] [17592186382066 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382067 13194139536068 true] [17592186382066 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382068 13194139536068 true] [17592186382066 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382069 13194139536068 true] [17592186382066 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382070 13194139536068 true] [17592186382066 1000 #edb/ref 17592186382071 13194139536068 true] [17592186382066 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139536068 true] [17592186382066 1041 "selection" 13194139536068 true] [17592186382066 1104 #edb/ref 17592186382069 13194139536068 true] [17592186382066 1110 #uuid "2e5f5d4b-ae66-5809-9811-870151c8154f" 13194139536068 true] [17592186382067 1034 "3" 13194139536068 true] [17592186382067 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536068 true] [17592186382067 1111 #uuid "8d0325e8-8b6c-586d-b83b-d336b14159f1" 13194139536068 true] [17592186382068 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139536068 true] [17592186382068 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536068 true] [17592186382068 1111 #uuid "a7c55d19-794a-5188-8bae-067da7ce929d" 13194139536068 true] [17592186382069 1034 "2" 13194139536068 true] [17592186382069 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139536068 true] [17592186382069 1111 #uuid "d20a6bb5-547c-5c0d-81f1-591bf52af8ae" 13194139536068 tr